#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be6d2ce2-f7cc-5a87-86d1-21f769dbd580" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Confidence Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a47bcb-1ece-5126-b658-82da8b2c4b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539544} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffb1421-5374-56b5-a2bb-9fb21023bbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539640} {:answer/id #uuid "e32bf06a-c78d-5f2e-a3f1-fa589a9d0e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539867} {:answer/id #uuid "f66c0a76-54b6-5957-a194-faca4954715b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539892} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f2e26f-5082-5bf5-b032-5423339dd37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e32bf06a-c78d-5f2e-a3f1-fa589a9d0e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539867} :answer-field/id #uuid "5298ca4d-ecde-5661-bd4e-59d6462c363f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5391d4bc-fe22-5a3b-85bc-6502aec7782e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159626" :question/problem "The weights of the wooden and steel chairs produced by a furniture company have standard deviations of $2\\,\\text{lb}$ and $1\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively. The company wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of weights for the wooden chairs is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of weights for the steel chairs. They construct a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the company sampled $100$ wooden chairs and $150$ steel chairs and found that their mean weights are $10\\,\\text{lb}$ and $9\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1\\pm 0.423.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that \n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=10-9=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{150}}≈0.423\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 0.423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1\\pm 0.423.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(1-0.423,\\,1+0.423)=(0.577,\\,1.423)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21507a23-f729-56aa-8e9c-66eb5823a2d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afb2e10-ecca-5296-b23a-fa3c6ff59dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pm 0.666" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539612} {:answer/id #uuid "b131442b-fa8e-59c6-a34b-54dfaa572ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pm 1.911" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539779} {:answer/id #uuid "b4633134-7243-5e28-9de0-0f5355178cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pm 1.563" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539789} {:answer/id #uuid "e593264d-28ab-56a2-a3df-c5c95f2634fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pm 1.356" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539869} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbef54b-4be0-5d86-b67e-1f0fa388f6ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pm 0.938" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e593264d-28ab-56a2-a3df-c5c95f2634fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pm 1.356" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539869} :answer-field/id #uuid "21552841-7d86-5c0f-9131-48f4014f0900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9f98bfe-eda2-5f27-b0c5-b96f1c4792b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-159634" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=58$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=86$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=34$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=43,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=34-43=-9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3.4}^{2}}{58}+\\frac{{4.9}^{2}}{86}}≈1.356\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -9\\pm 1.356\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be6d2ce2-f7cc-5a87-86d1-21f769dbd580" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Confidence Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f2c1af-4f10-592c-9fcf-8c3faa46018b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539648} {:answer/id #uuid "73cbb3cd-94a4-531b-8992-a4d90c29793d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539672} {:answer/id #uuid "a14ed575-9de2-5d57-8f0a-151dcd5cb36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539749} {:answer/id #uuid "d94056d5-23c1-5895-8352-362b6172cbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539854} {:answer/id #uuid "f83320d5-06d8-528d-a2fa-782e2f50780c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d94056d5-23c1-5895-8352-362b6172cbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539854} :answer-field/id #uuid "f24e3ec2-08cb-5869-8ee2-41217f338f4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34a9b0ce-4c8f-5634-aaa8-208faeda7581" :question/math-academy-id "q-159641" :question/problem "It is known that the thickness of ceramic tiles from suppliers X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=1.6\\,\\text{mm}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=1.2\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively. A construction contractor wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier Y. He constructs a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the contractor sampled $20$ tiles from supplier X and $18$ tiles from supplier Y and found that their mean thicknesses are $18.8\\,\\text{mm}$ and $18.5\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=18.8-18.5=0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{1.6}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18}}≈0.894\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 0.894\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(0.3-0.894,\\,0.3+0.894)=(-0.594,\\,1.194)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21ac91dd-ea0e-5432-8431-f5fa4d32c6bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bb24e5f-7d8c-5eb1-90c8-8d9b94a92255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pm 3.760" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539558} {:answer/id #uuid "9dba8f44-3248-5a69-9eee-19fc5e572be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pm 6.784" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539739} {:answer/id #uuid "b29e678d-3c58-5275-b969-0ca48fcef8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pm 4.580" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539785} {:answer/id #uuid "b62c0cb8-6799-5ac4-bb8e-3e9824262e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pm 4.530" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539797} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4dc5f0-036a-5939-ad15-8aa21a1aa5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pm 6.380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec4dc5f0-036a-5939-ad15-8aa21a1aa5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pm 6.380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539877} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe7bf0ba-27c6-5e85-90fc-d44843ac230f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12431c38-5ac6-59aa-ba15-2add6471e563" :question/math-academy-id "q-159642" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=30$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=22$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-16=6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{32}}≈6.380\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 6\\pm 6.380\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21ac91dd-ea0e-5432-8431-f5fa4d32c6bb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e34c82e-f3f4-523a-ab51-ab809db9fcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 3.238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539591} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3f9768-135a-5874-97de-788770ddf0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 3.118" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539663} {:answer/id #uuid "99447647-4006-52bb-adcd-1816c068b843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pm 2.832" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539733} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb298de-a5f9-5a47-b8be-adf5a2151ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 3.442" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539742} {:answer/id #uuid "f4288d34-1117-5ed0-b243-2c2c810cc589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pm 2.768" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e3f9768-135a-5874-97de-788770ddf0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pm 3.118" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539663} :answer-field/id #uuid "736c5de7-2fbd-5a65-8262-e8bce4536075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db9cf9a1-6584-5405-8a36-efa72137f427" :question/math-academy-id "q-159643" :question/problem "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=28$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=19$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=11,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=19-11=8\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{32}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{28}}≈3.118\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 8\\pm 3.118\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
